      <p class="mb-1" style="font-family:times;font-weight:bold;"><strong>A</strong></p>
      <blockquote class="mb-1" style="padding:0px 0px 0px 10px;font-size:13px;">
        <ul class="pl-0 ml-3 mb-0" style="font-size:13px;">
        <li>Ababil — (arabic) "a flock of birds", also means martin bird / swallow in some versions.</li>
        <li>Abaddon — the destoyer or angel of bottomless pit; hell; the bottomless pit; a place of destruction. from the late latin "abaddōn", from the ancient greek ʾāβαδδών (abaddṓn), from the biblical hebrew אבדון (ʾăḇaddōn, literally "destruction, abyss"), from אבד (ʾāḇaḏ, "to be lost, to perish").</li>
        <li>Abbandonato — (italian) "abandoned".</li>
        <li>Abreuvoir — (french) "a watering place", "water fountain".</li>
        <li>Abronia — a genus of plants commonly known as sand-verbenas.</li>
        <li>Absatzzeichen — (german) pilcrow, paragraph mark, alinea mark.</li>
        <li>Absquatulate — (verb) "leave abruptly".</li>
        <li>Acacia — a large genus of shrubs and trees in the subfamily mimosoideae of the pea family fabaceae.</li>
        <li>Acanthus — a genus containing plants used for ornament and in traditional medicine.</li>
        <li>Acedia — from latin acēdia, and from greek ἀκηδία, "negligence", ἀ- "lack of" -κηδία "care". variously defined as a state of listlessness or torpor, of not caring or not being concerned with one's position or condition in the world.</li>
        <li>Achlys — an ancient greek goddess who symbolizes the mist of death.</li>
        <li>Achtung — (german) "attention", "warning".</li>
        <li>Acid — a molecule or ion capable of donating a proton.</li>
        <li>Acrasia — (archaic, rare) lack of self-control; excess, intemperance. also means irregular or unruly behavior. learned borrowing from late latin acrasia ("lack of temperance") and from its etymon ancient greek ᾰ̓κρᾱσῐ́ᾱ (akrāsíā, "bad mixture").</li>
        <li>Adagio — "to play slowly", "at a slow tempo".</li>
        <li>Adamastor — a mythological character created by poet luís de camões as a personification of the cape of good hope, symbolizing the dangers of the sea and the formidable forces of nature challenged and ultimately overcome by the portuguese during the age of discovery.</li>
        <li>Adhafera — zeta leonis, a third-magnitude star in the constellation of leo, the lion.</li>
        <li>Adiemus — resembles the latin word "adeamus", meaning "let us approach" (or "let us submit a cause to a referee").</li>
        <li>Adon — literally means "lord." it has an uncertain etymology, although it is generally believed to be derived from the ugaritic "ad" (father). the pluralization of "adon" (my lord) is "adonai" (my lords). it is common religious practice to restrict the use of the word "adonai" to prayer only.</li>
        <li>Aechmea — a genus of flowering plants in the family bromeliaceae (subfamily bromelioideae). the name comes from the greek "aichme", meaning "spear".</li>
        <li>Aeneid — (latin) a latin epic poem that tells the legendary story of aeneas.</li>
        <li>Aerangis — a genus of the orchid family (orchidaceae). derived from the greek words "aer" (air) and "angos" (urn), referring to the form of the lip.</li>
        <li>Aeranthes — an orchid genus, means "aerial flower", because it grows high in the air.</li>
        <li>Aeschylus — an ancient greek author of greek tragedy, and is often described as the father of tragedy. legend says he died from having a tortoise dropped onto his head by an eagle.</li>
        <li>Affogato — italian coffee-based dessert.</li>
        <li>Agapanthus — lily of the nile, derived from greek "agapē" (love) and "anthos" (flower).</li>
        <li>Aggripa — a praenomen (personal name). exact meaning is unknown, speculated that it was originally used for a child who was born feet-first (a breech birth)- though it is probably a false etymology.</li>
        <li>Aggrovigliare — (italian) transitive verb, "to tangle", "to muddle up", "to complicate".</li>
        <li>Agno — from latin agnus, an obsolete and poetic form meaning "lamb".</li>
        <li>Agon — a struggle or contest; conflict; especially between the protagonist and antagonist in a literary work.</li>
        <li>Agrippina — mother of the roman emperor nero and a powerful influence on him during the early years of his reign; latin, "born feetfirst".</li>
        <li>Ahasuerus / Ahasveros — a name applied in the hebrew bible to three rulers of ancient persia and to a babylonian official (or median king) in the book of tobit.</li>
        <li>Ahitophel — a counselor of king david and a man greatly renowned for his wisdom. during absalom's attempt to usurp the throne, he deserted david and supported absalom, whom he then turned to as an advisor.</li>
        <li>Aidos — the greek term for the great shame felt by a hero after failure.</li>
        <li>Aiguillette — from french word "aiguille", "needle". in the context of butchery, means any meat cut into long, thin strips. in modern usage, an ornamental braided cord with decorative metal tips worn on uniforms.</li>
        <li>Alabaster — a stone of great purity and whiteness, used for ornaments. the name is said to be from an egyptian town, alabastron, but nothing is known of this town nor the ultimate origin of the greek word.</li>
